     {
      "type": "spacer",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "id": "/content/mobile/lh/en/articles/essentials-of-barcelona/jcr:content/articlecontentfragment/par3/separator_copy_copy__716010515"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "<b>Better Beach Days</b>",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_4"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "Beaches in Barcelona? Before the 1990s, that was just wishful thinking. In preparation for the 1992 Olympic Games, 4.5 kilometers of industrial coastline were transformed into beautiful sandy beaches, such as Bogatell and Sant Sebastià, which have been loved by both locals and tourists ever since.",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_5"
     },
     {
      "type": "image",
      "id": "/content/mobile/lh/en/articles/essentials-of-barcelona/jcr:content/articlecontentfragment/par5/image",
      "url": "https://www.lufthansa.com/content/dam/lh/article-images/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona/lh_blog_barcelona_illustration2_sylvia-neuner_1920x1080.jpg.transform/lh-dcep-transform-width-1440/img.jpg",
      "contentDescription": "Illustration on the left: Barcelona’s beaches being created with a bucket of sand. Illustration on the right: the Sagrada Família on the back of a snail."
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "<b>Monumental Construction Time</b>",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_6"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "The Sagrada Família, Gaudí's church masterpiece, remains unfinished. Construction began in 1882, but over 50 years later, at the height of the Spanish Civil War, the building plans were nearly abandoned when revolutionaries destroyed Gaudí's plaster models. It took 16 years just to reconstruct them. The Sagrada Família is now expected to be completed in 2026.",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_7"
     },
     {
      "type": "image",
      "id": "/content/mobile/lh/en/articles/essentials-of-barcelona/jcr:content/articlecontentfragment/par7/image",
      "url": "https://www.lufthansa.com/content/dam/lh/article-images/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona/lh_blog_barcelona_illustration3_sylvia-neuner_1920x1080.jpg.transform/lh-dcep-transform-width-1440/img.jpg",
      "contentDescription": "Illustration: A footballer pumping up the Camp Nou stadium as if it were a tire"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "<b>To New Heights</b>",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_8"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "Camp Nou, home of FC Barcelona, is the largest stadium in Europe. Opened in 1957, it seats 99,354 spectators and houses a museum showcasing the club’s history. It’s currently undergoing major renovations and is set to hold 105,000 spectators upon completion.",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_9"
     },
     {
      "type": "copyText",
      "text": "All illustrations © Sylvia Neuner",
      "size": "MEDIUM",
      "variant": "BODY",
      "id": "/lh/en/mobile-app/europe/essentials-of-barcelona_paragraph_10"
     }
